(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-02"] = {"2.1":{"s":"A solution is a uniform mixture of two or more substances. The one present in larger amount is the solvent; the others are solutes. Air, sea water and alloys are solutions. This chapter covers how much dissolves, vapour pressure of solutions, and colligative properties.","x":["A solution is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances, which looks the same all through. The substance present in the larger amount is the solvent, and the others are solutes. Sugar in water, air (a mixture of gases), and brass (a solid solution of zinc in copper) are all solutions.","In this chapter we learn how to express the concentration of a solution, how gases and solids dissolve, what happens to the vapour pressure of a mixture of liquids, and the colligative properties, which depend only on the number of dissolved particles. These properties let us find molar masses and explain osmosis."],"e":"Sea water is a solution of salts in water. It freezes at a lower temperature and boils at a higher temperature than pure water, which is one of the colligative effects of this chapter."},"2.2":{"s":"Solute and solvent can each be a solid, a liquid or a gas, so there are nine types of solution. Oxygen in water is gas in liquid. Alcohol in water is liquid in liquid. Sugar in water is solid in liquid. Hydrogen in palladium is gas in solid, and brass is solid in solid.","x":["Solutions are classified by the physical state of the solvent and the solute, so there are nine possible types. The solvent decides the state of the solution.","Gaseous solutions: gas in gas (air), liquid in gas (water vapour in air), solid in gas (camphor vapour in air). Liquid solutions: gas in liquid (carbon dioxide in soda water), liquid in liquid (ethanol in water), solid in liquid (sugar in water). Solid solutions: gas in solid (hydrogen in palladium), liquid in solid (mercury in sodium, an amalgam), solid in solid (alloys such as brass and bronze).","Concentration tells how much solute there is. Common ways to express it: mass percentage (grams of solute in 100 g of solution), parts per million (for very small amounts), mole fraction x = moles of one component/total moles (the fractions add up to 1), molarity M = moles of solute per litre of solution (changes with temperature), and molality m = moles of solute per kilogram of solvent (does not change with temperature)."],"e":"A solution has 1 mole of ethanol in 9 moles of water. The mole fraction of ethanol is 1/10 = 0.1, and that of water is 0.9."},"2.3":{"s":"As you add solute, it dissolves quickly at first and then more slowly. Finally no more dissolves: the solution is saturated, and dissolving and crystallising happen at the same rate. Whether a solid dissolves depends on how strong the solute-solvent attractions are compared with those it must break.","x":["A solvent cannot dissolve an unlimited amount of a solute. When a solute is added to a solvent, it dissolves until no more will go in at that temperature. Such a solution is called saturated. A solution that can still dissolve more solute is unsaturated.","In a saturated solution, there is a dynamic equilibrium: the rate at which solute dissolves equals the rate at which it comes out as crystals. The concentration of a saturated solution is the solubility.","A solution which holds more solute than it should at that temperature is supersaturated. It is unstable, and a small crystal of the solute (a seed) makes the extra solute crystallise suddenly. The saying 'like dissolves like' is a good rule: polar solvents, such as water, dissolve polar or ionic solutes, and non-polar solvents, such as benzene, dissolve non-polar solutes such as fats and iodine."],"e":"At 25 °C about 36 g of sodium chloride dissolves in 100 g of water. A solution with 36 g in 100 g is saturated. It cannot take any more."},"2.4":{"s":"Solubility is the amount of solute in a saturated solution at a given temperature, usually in mol L⁻¹. 'Like dissolves like': polar solutes dissolve in polar solvents. Temperature changes solubility. Gases become less soluble on heating. Henry's law: a gas's solubility is proportional to its pressure above the liquid, which is why soda fizzes when opened.","f":[{"x":"S = K_H*P","n":"Henry's law: solubility of a gas (mol/L) is proportional to its pressure P (bar); K_H in mol L^-1 bar^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.4, p.30"}],"w":["NH₃ and CO₂ dissolve more than Henry's law predicts because they react with water.","Gases dissolve LESS in hot water; many solids dissolve more."],"x":["Solubility is the maximum amount of solute that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a given temperature. It depends on the nature of the solute and the solvent, the temperature and, for gases, the pressure.","For most solids in liquids, dissolving takes in heat (endothermic), so solubility increases with temperature. Some (like sodium sulphate above 32 °C or calcium hydroxide) behave in the opposite way. Pressure has hardly any effect on the solubility of solids and liquids.","For a gas in a liquid the solubility falls when the temperature rises, since dissolving a gas gives out heat. It rises when the pressure of the gas increases. Henry's law states this: the solubility S of a gas is proportional to the pressure p of the gas above the liquid, S = k_H p. A gas with a higher k_H has a lower solubility.","Applications: soft drinks are bottled under pressure so more CO₂ dissolves, and it fizzes when you open the bottle. Divers breathing air at depth have more nitrogen dissolved in their blood, and a rapid rise can form bubbles (the bends). Warm lakes hold less oxygen, which harms fish."],"e":"If 0.0014 mol/L of a gas dissolves in water at 1 atm, then at 3 atm and the same temperature, 0.0042 mol/L dissolves."},"2.5":{"s":"Over a mixture of two volatile liquids, each one adds its own vapour pressure. Raoult's law: the partial vapour pressure of each component equals its mole fraction times the vapour pressure of the pure liquid. Ideal solutions obey it exactly. Real solutions can show positive or negative deviations, depending on whether the new attractions are weaker or stronger.","f":[{"x":"P_1 = x_1*P_1^0","n":"Raoult's law: partial vapour pressure of each volatile component; x = mole fraction, P^0 = pure vapour pressure.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.5, p.31"},{"x":"P = (P_2^0 - P_1^0)*x_2 + P_1^0","n":"Total vapour pressure of an ideal binary solution: a straight line in x_2.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.5, p.31"}],"w":["Positive deviation: weaker attractions in the mixture (ethanol + acetone). Negative deviation: stronger (chloroform + acetone)."],"x":["Liquids evaporate. In a closed vessel the vapour above the liquid exerts a pressure called the vapour pressure, which depends on the liquid and the temperature, and is higher when the liquid evaporates easily.","For a solution of two volatile liquids A and B, Raoult's law says that the partial vapour pressure of each component is proportional to its mole fraction in the liquid: p_A = x_A p_A° and p_B = x_B p_B°, where the p° are the vapour pressures of the pure liquids. The total pressure is p = p_A + p_B. The vapour is richer in the more volatile component.","A solution that follows Raoult's law at all concentrations is ideal. It needs the A–B attractions to be the same as the A–A and B–B ones. For such a solution ΔH and ΔV of mixing are zero. Benzene with toluene, and hexane with heptane, are nearly ideal.","Real solutions deviate. If the A–B attractions are weaker (ethanol and water, acetone and carbon disulphide), the vapour pressure is higher than predicted: positive deviation. If they are stronger (acetone and chloroform, HNO₃ and water), the vapour pressure is lower: negative deviation. Such mixtures may form azeotropes, which boil at a fixed temperature and cannot be separated by fractional distillation. A positive deviation gives a minimum-boiling azeotrope (ethanol–water, 95.6% ethanol). A negative deviation gives a maximum-boiling one (HNO₃–water, 68% HNO₃)."],"e":"A mixture of equal moles of two liquids with p° = 80 kPa and 40 kPa has partial pressures 40 kPa and 20 kPa, a total of 60 kPa. The vapour has x = 40/60 = 0.67 of the more volatile liquid."},"2.6":{"s":"Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles, not on what they are. There are four: relative lowering of vapour pressure, boiling point elevation, freezing point depression and osmotic pressure. Measuring any of them lets you find the molar mass of the solute.","w":["Colligative properties depend on the NUMBER of solute particles, not on what they are."],"x":["Some properties of dilute solutions depend only on the number of solute particles dissolved in a fixed amount of solvent, and not on what the solute is. They are the colligative properties. There are four of them. The vapour pressure drops, the boiling point goes up, the freezing point goes down, and the solution develops an osmotic pressure.","The reason is that solute particles in the surface of the liquid take up space, so fewer solvent molecules can escape. As a result, the vapour pressure falls, the solution has to be heated more to boil, and it must be cooled more to freeze.","These properties are accurate for dilute solutions of non-electrolytes, and they are used to find the molar mass of a solute, particularly large molecules such as polymers and proteins, since the measured effect is proportional to the number of moles of solute, so n = mass/molar mass can be solved for the molar mass.","For solutes that ionise or associate, the number of particles is not the same as the number of formula units. This is corrected with the van't Hoff factor i (topic 2.11)."],"e":"0.1 mol of glucose and 0.1 mol of urea, each in 1 kg of water, lower the freezing point by the same amount (about 0.186 K), although the substances are different."},"2.7":{"s":"A non-volatile solute takes up part of the surface, so fewer solvent molecules escape and the vapour pressure falls. The relative lowering of vapour pressure, (P° − P)/P°, equals the mole fraction of the solute.","f":[{"x":"(P_1^0 - P_1)/P_1^0 = x_2","n":"Relative lowering of vapour pressure = mole fraction of the nonvolatile solute.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.7, p.34"},{"x":"(P_1^0 - P_1)/P_1^0 = (W_2*M_1)/(M_2*W_1)","n":"Dilute solution; W = mass, M = molar mass (1 = solvent, 2 = solute).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.7, p.35"}],"x":["When a non-volatile solute (such as sugar or salt) is dissolved in a volatile solvent, the solution gives off less vapour than the pure solvent would at the same temperature. Only the solvent evaporates, and the solute particles occupy a part of the surface.","By Raoult's law, p = x_solvent p°. The lowering of vapour pressure is p° − p = p°(1 − x_solvent) = p° x_solute. So the relative lowering of vapour pressure is (p° − p)/p° = x_solute, the mole fraction of the solute.","For a dilute solution with n moles of solute and N moles of solvent, x_solute = n/(n + N) which is close to n/N. Putting n = w/M and N = W/M₀ (the masses and molar masses of the solute and the solvent) gives (p° − p)/p° = wM₀/(MW). If the other quantities are known, the molar mass M of the solute can be calculated.","A solution of a non-volatile solute therefore always has a lower vapour pressure, and the lowering is larger when the solute is more concentrated."],"e":"Pure water has p° = 760 mmHg at 100 °C. A solute with mole fraction 0.1 gives p = 0.9 × 760 = 684 mmHg. The lowering is 76 mmHg."},"2.8":{"s":"A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals the outside pressure. A solute lowers the vapour pressure, so a higher temperature is needed: the boiling point rises. ΔTb = Kb × m, where m is the molality and Kb is the ebullioscopic constant of the solvent.","f":[{"x":"Delta T_b = K_b*m","n":"Boiling point elevation; m = molality, K_b = ebullioscopic constant (K kg mol^-1).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.8, p.36"},{"x":"M_2 = 1000*K_b*W_2/(Delta T_b*W_1)","n":"Molar mass of solute; W_1 = mass of solvent in grams.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.8, p.37"}],"w":["Use molality (per kg of solvent), not molarity: it does not change with temperature.","A temperature difference is the same in K and in °C."],"x":["A liquid boils when its vapour pressure becomes equal to the external pressure. A solution of a non-volatile solute has a lower vapour pressure than the pure solvent at every temperature, so it must be heated to a higher temperature to reach the external pressure. The boiling point goes up. The difference is the elevation of boiling point, ΔT_b = T_b − T_b°.","For a dilute solution, ΔT_b is proportional to the molality m: ΔT_b = K_b m. K_b is the molal elevation constant (or ebullioscopic constant). It depends only on the solvent, and it is the elevation for a 1 molal solution. Its unit is K kg/mol. For water, K_b = 0.52 K kg/mol, and for benzene, 2.53.","Using m = (w/M)/(W/1000) with w the mass of the solute and W the mass of the solvent in grams, the molar mass is M = 1000 K_b w/(ΔT_b W). This is a convenient way to find M from a measurement of the boiling point."],"e":"A 0.5 molal solution of glucose in water boils at 100 + 0.52 × 0.5 = 100.26 °C."},"2.9":{"s":"A solution freezes at a lower temperature than the pure solvent. ΔTf = Kf × m, where Kf is the cryoscopic constant. This is why salt melts ice on roads and antifreeze protects car radiators.","f":[{"x":"Delta T_f = K_f*m","n":"Freezing point depression; K_f = cryoscopic constant.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.9, p.38"},{"x":"M_2 = 1000*K_f*W_2/(Delta T_f*W_1)","n":"Molar mass of solute from freezing point depression.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.9, p.38"}],"x":["A liquid freezes when its vapour pressure equals that of the solid. A solution of a non-volatile solute has a lower vapour pressure than the pure solvent, and the vapour pressure curve of the solution meets that of the solid solvent at a lower temperature. So the freezing point falls. The depression of freezing point is ΔT_f = T_f° − T_f.","For a dilute solution ΔT_f = K_f m. K_f is the molal depression constant (the cryoscopic constant), which depends only on the solvent. For water, K_f = 1.86 K kg/mol, and for benzene, 5.12. The molar mass of the solute is M = 1000 K_f w/(ΔT_f W).","Uses: antifreeze (ethylene glycol) in a car radiator lowers the freezing point of the cooling water. Salt on icy roads melts ice, and a mixture of ice and salt is used as a freezing mixture, as it can reach about −21 °C. Camphor (K_f = 39.7) and other solvents with a large K_f are used when finding the molar mass of a solute, because they give a large and easily measured depression."],"e":"3.0 g of urea (60 g/mol) in 100 g of water gives m = 0.5 and ΔT_f = 1.86 × 0.5 = 0.93 K. The solution freezes at −0.93 °C."},"2.10":{"s":"Osmosis is the flow of solvent through a semipermeable membrane from a dilute solution into a concentrated one. Osmotic pressure is the pressure needed to stop it: π = CRT, like the gas equation. Isotonic solutions have equal osmotic pressure. Applying more pressure than π reverses the flow (reverse osmosis), which is used to purify water.","f":[{"x":"pi = M*R*T","n":"Osmotic pressure; M = molarity, R = 0.08206 L atm K^-1 mol^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.10, p.40"},{"x":"M_2 = W_2*R*T/(pi*V)","n":"Molar mass of solute; V in litres.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.10, p.40"}],"w":["Isotonic solutions have equal osmotic pressure; solvent flows from hypotonic to hypertonic.","Osmotic pressure is the best method for large molecules (proteins, polymers): the effect is large."],"x":["Osmosis is the flow of solvent molecules through a semipermeable membrane (one that lets the solvent through but not the solute) from a dilute solution, or pure solvent, into a more concentrated solution. The solvent moves to even out the concentrations.","The osmotic pressure π is the extra pressure that must be applied to the solution to stop the flow of solvent into it. For a dilute solution of concentration C (mol/L) the van't Hoff equation gives π = CRT, which is like the ideal gas law πV = nRT. With R = 0.0821 L atm/(mol K), π comes out in atm.","Two solutions with the same osmotic pressure at the same temperature are isotonic. A solution with a higher osmotic pressure is hypertonic, and one with a lower pressure is hypotonic. A red blood cell in a hypertonic solution loses water and shrinks. In a hypotonic one it takes in water and swells, and may burst. Normal saline (0.9% NaCl) is isotonic with blood.","If a pressure larger than π is applied to the solution side, the solvent flows out of the solution, in the opposite direction. This is reverse osmosis, used to make drinking water from sea water. Osmotic pressure is a particularly good way to find the molar mass of large molecules, since even a very dilute solution gives a measurable π."],"e":"A solution of 0.01 mol/L at 300 K has π = 0.01 × 0.0821 × 300 = 0.246 atm, which is a column of water about 2.5 m high."},"2.11":{"s":"Electrolytes split into ions, so they give more particles than their formula suggests and larger colligative effects. The van't Hoff factor i is the observed effect divided by the expected one: about 2 for NaCl, about 3 for CaCl₂. Each formula above is multiplied by i.","f":[{"x":"i = (Delta T_f)/(Delta T_f)_0","n":"van't Hoff factor: observed colligative property over that expected for no dissociation; also = M_theoretical/M_observed.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.11, p.42"},{"x":"Delta T_f = i*K_f*m","n":"Electrolytes: each colligative formula is multiplied by i (likewise Delta T_b, pi = i M R T, Delta P).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.11, p.42"},{"x":"alpha = (i - 1)/(n - 1)","n":"Degree of dissociation; n = ions from one formula unit.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 2.11, p.43"}],"w":["i ≈ 2 for NaCl and KCl, ≈ 3 for CaCl₂ and Na₂SO₄ in very dilute solution; i < 1 means association.","Abnormal (low) molar mass from colligative data means the solute has dissociated."],"x":["The formulas above assume that the solute stays as single particles. Electrolytes such as NaCl, in water, split into ions, so one formula unit gives two or more particles, and the colligative effect is larger than expected. Some solutes associate: acetic acid in benzene forms dimers, so the number of particles is smaller and the effect is smaller.","The van't Hoff factor i corrects for this: i = (observed colligative property)/(calculated value assuming no change), or equivalently i = (number of particles actually present)/(number of formula units dissolved) = (normal molar mass)/(observed molar mass). The formulas become ΔT_b = iK_b m, ΔT_f = iK_f m and π = iCRT, and the relative lowering of vapour pressure is also multiplied by i.","For complete dissociation, i equals the number of ions: 2 for NaCl and KCl, 3 for K₂SO₄ and CaCl₂, 4 for AlCl₃ and K₃[Fe(CN)₆] (three K⁺ ions and one complex ion), and 5 for K₄[Fe(CN)₆]. For a solute that does not ionise (glucose, urea, sucrose), i = 1. For association (acetic acid in benzene forming dimers), i is about 0.5.","For a partly dissociated electrolyte, with degree of dissociation α and n ions per formula unit, i = 1 + (n − 1)α. In real solutions, i is a bit less than the ideal value, since the ions attract each other, and the effect is larger in more concentrated solutions."],"e":"0.1 molal NaCl (i close to 2) lowers the freezing point of water by about 2 × 1.86 × 0.1 = 0.372 K, twice the effect of 0.1 molal glucose."}};
